  "text": "Hannah prayed so hard the priest thought she was drunk. First Samuel chapter one. Hannah is one of two wives, and she has no children, and Peninnah, the one with children, provokes her about it. The text calls Peninnah her adversary, and says this happened year after year, every time they went up to the house of the Lord. So Hannah stops eating and she weeps. Her husband Elkanah loves her, and he says the most well meaning wrong thing in Scripture. Am not I better to thee than ten sons? Then she gets up from the table and goes to the temple, and she prays in bitterness of soul. And here is the detail. She spake in her heart, only her lips moved, but her voice was not heard. Eli the priest is watching her mouth, and he decides she is drunk, and he says so out loud. Put away thy wine from thee. And Hannah does not shrink and she does not fight. No, my lord. I am a woman of a sorrowful spirit. I have poured out my soul before the Lord. She was the most honest person in that building, and the professional called her a drunk.",
